6 17. Qxh6 Rxb4) Bxh6  (12... Nd3+ {the takeaway is that it's important to be tactically aware during the opening phase, tactics are always alive. }  13. Kf1 Bxh6 14. Qxh6 c6)  (12... Ncxe4!! {[%cal Gd8h4]this tactical sequence is absolutely absurd and franky not very human}  13. Bxe4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 Qh4 15. Bxg7 Qxe4+ 16. Ne2 Kxg7) 13. Qxh6 Nd3+ 14. Kf1 c6 {[%cal Gb7c7]immediately breaking open the centre. black sacrifices a pawn to keep the king awkwardly placed. })  (11... Bd7  ) 12. g4 {[%eval -0.07] }  (12. O-O-O  ) Ng8?! {[%eval +1.00] Inaccuracy. c6 was best. preparing f5 and defending h6, I thought this was quite logical, but it's slightly too slow. Better was c6! immediately striking the centre when he attacks on the flank and not being scared of g5 ideas. I think ng8 is also bad because the knight on f6 was preventing h5 by attacking g4, once the knight went back h5 can be played without losing the g4 pawn. }  (12... c6!  13. dxc6  (13. h4 {what if white just ignores c6? }  cxd5 14. Nxd5  (14. cxd5  ) Nfxe4)  (13. g5 {tactically, this isn't good for white because of the weak e4 pawn}  hxg5 14. Bxg5 cxd5 15. Nxd5  (15. cxd5  b5 16. Nxb5 Rb8 17. a4 Nb3) Nfxe4 16. Bxe4 Nxe4) bxc6 14. Bxc5 dxc5 15. Qxd8 Rxd8 16. Rd1 Rd4) 13. h4! {[%eval +1.01] }  (13. Bxc5  )  (13. O-O-O  a4 14. h4 f5 15. gxf5 gxf5 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Nge2 f4 18. Nb5 Nf6 19. Nec3 c6 20. Na3 h5 21. Qc2 Bd7 22. Nxa4 cxd5 23. exd5+ e4 24. Nxc5) f5 {[%eval +1.15] } 14. exf5 {[%eval +1.13] }  (14. h5  fxg4) gxf5 {[%eval +1.26] } 15. gxf5?! {[%eval +0.52] Inaccuracy. Bxc5 was best.}  (15. Bxc5  dxc5 16. g5 a4 17. a3 e4 18. Bh5 Qd6 19. Nh3 Bd7 20. O-O-O Rab8) Bxf5 {[%eval +0.59] } 16. Bxc5 {[%eval +0.58] } dxc5 {[%eval +0.50] } 17. Be4 {[%eval +0.49] [%cal Gh7h8]} Ne7?! {[%eval +1.33] Inaccuracy. Kh8 was best. a seemingly logical move, developing and defending my bishop, but it was better to go kh8 rather than kg8, where the king is shielded from any g file attacks. In the game I thought that my rook on f5 after Bxf5 would have been misplaced, but I shouldn't have worried as there is no actual good way for white to take advantage of this and attack the rook, also it helps me double on the f file. }  (17... Kh8  18. Qd3  (18. O-O-O  Bxe4 19. Nxe4 Nf6 20. Ng5 hxg5 21. hxg5+ Nh7 22. g6 Rf4 23. Rxh7+)  (18. Bxf5  Rxf5 {black shouldn't be afraid of putting his rook on f5. it's an active square and can easily slide back if threatened and potentially even go to d4} 19. Nge2 Nf6 20. Ng3 Rf4)  (18. f3  Qd7 19. Qc2 Ne7) Ne7 19. Nge2 Qd7 20. Ng3 Bxe4 21. Ncxe4  (21. Qxe4  Rf4 22. Qe2 Raf8 23. Nce4 Nf5 24. Nxc5 Qf7  (24... Qe7  ) 25. Ne6 Nd4 26. Nxd4 exd4 27. Qh5  (27. O-O-O  Kg8  (27... Re8  28. Qh5 Rxf2 29. Qxf7  (29. Rhg1  Qf4+ 30. Kb1 Re3 31. Qg6 d3  (31... Qe5  ) 32. Qxg7+ Kxg7 33. Nh5+ Kf7 34. Nxf4 Rxf4 35. Rdf1 Re4 36. b3 Kf6 37. Rc1  (37. Kc1  )  (37. Kb2  Rxf1))) 28. Qh5))) 18. Qd3 {[%eval +0.84] } Kg8?! {[%eval +1.44] Inaccuracy. Kh8 was best. Kg8 just stays on the g file and the rook may come in to the attaack. The king is very safe on h8. I'm not sure why I choise g8. }  (18... Kh8  19. O-O-O Rf7 20. Nge2 Qf8 21. Rdf1 Bxe4 22. Nxe4 Rf3 23. Qc2 b6 24. a3) 19. Nge2 {[%eval +1.47] } Qd7 {[%eval +1.42] } 20. Ng3 {[%eval +0.96] } Bxe4 {[%eval +1.00] }  (20... Rf7  ) 21. Qxe4? {[%eval -0.26] Mistake. Ncxe4 was best.}  (21. Ncxe4  Qg4 22. Qe2 Qxe2+ 23. Nxe2 b6 24. O-O-O Rad8 25. h5 Nc8 26. Rhg1 Nd6) Rf4! {[%eval -0.30] } 22. Qe2 {[%eval -0.38] } Raf8 {[%eval -0.08] } 23. Nce4?! {[%eval -0.81] Inaccuracy. Nge4 was best.}  (23. Nge4  Nf5 24. Nxc5 Qe8 25. O-O-O Nd4 26. Qe3 a4 27. Rhg1 a3 28. Kb1 b6) Nf5 {[%eval -0.95] } 24. Nxc5? {[%eval -2.26] Mistake. O-O-O was best.}  (24. O-O-O  Nd4 25. Qd3 Rf3 26. Qd2 a4 27. Nxc5 Qg4 28. Nge4 R3f4 29. Rde1 a3) Qf7?? {[%eval -0.10] Blunder. Qe7 was best. so I actually thought Qe7 was a good move and spent a long time while he was thinking justifying it, but then I saw Qf7 and thought it was even stronger, tripling on the f file while threatening nd4. I also played Qf7 instantly, which was due to my ego and wanting to show him that I calculated it all out. But I really should have spent atleast 10 minutes here justifying Qf7 or Qe7. The problem with Qf7 is that white now has time to long castle out of immediate danger, whereas Qe7 immediately attacks the knight and threatens Qb4+. Also, it allows white Qh5 to threaten the queen trade.}  (24... Qe7  25. Nxf5 Qxc5 26. Ne3 Qb4+ 27. Kf1 Rxf2+ 28. Qxf2 Rxf2+ 29. Kxf2 e4 30. Kg3) 25. Ne6? {[%eval -1.40] Mistake. O-O-O was best.}  (25. O-O-O  Nd4 26. Qh5 Rxf2 27. Qxf7+ R8xf7 28. Kb1 b6 29. Ne6 Re7 30. Ne4 Rf3) Nd4 {[%eval -1.23] } 26. Nxd4 {[%eval -1.30] [%cal Gf4f2]} exd4?! {[%eval -0.60] Inaccuracy. Rxf2 was best. Another move I made too quickly, completely ignoring his possible responses and only focusing in on my own attack. Rxf7 was crushing here and I should have seized the advantage. }  (26... Rxf2  27. Qh5  (27. Qg4  exd4 {and white has nothing at all, his king is in the centre and my king is a lot more safe then his. } 28. O-O-O d3) exd4 28. Qxf7+ R8xf7 29. O-O-O d3 30. Rd2 R2f3 31. Ne4 R7f4 32. Re1) 27. O-O-O {[%eval -0.53] } Re8 {[%eval -0.08] trying to prevent the queen trade but completely missing Qh5 which I saw as soon as I made the move. The main takeaway is that you must lookg for opponent responses just as hard as you look for your own. } 28. Qh5 {[%eval -0.17] } Rxf2 {[%eval -0.11] } 29. Rhg1?? {[%eval -3.20] Blunder. Qxf7+ was best.}  (29. Qxf7+  Rxf7 30. Rdf1 Ref8 31. Rxf7 Rxf7 32. Rg1 Kh7 33. h5 Be5 34. Rf1 Rxf1+) Qf4+ {[%eval -3.17] } 30. Kb1 {[%eval -2.81] } Re3?? {[%eval 0.00] Blunder. Re7 was best. Again, the common theme in this game is that I wasn't seeing my opponent's good responses properly, or I wasn't looking out for them hard enough. I completely missed Qg6 which defends the knight, Re2! was much stronger, defending the bishop and killing any discovered attack threats, while d3 threats still linger in the air. }  (30... Re7 {[%csl Gf4][%cal Gd4d3,Gg7b2,Gf2b2]Now white just has too many problems to deal with}  31. Nh1 Rfe2 32. d6 Qxd6 33. Qb5 R7e3 34. Rdf1 d3 35. Nf2 d2 36. Qf5) 31. Qg6 {[%eval -0.10] } d3 {[%eval -0.01] } 32. Qxg7+ {[%eval 0.00] } Kxg7 {[%eval 0.00] } 33. Nh5+ {[%eval 0.00] } Kf7 {[%eval 0.00] } 34. Nxf4 {[%eval 0.00] } Rxf4 {[%eval 0.00] } 35. Rdf1 {[%eval -0.03] } Re4 {[%eval 0.00] } 36. b3 {[%eval 0.00] } Kf6 {[%eval 0.00] } 37. Kc1?? {[%eval -3.05] Blunder. Kb2 was best.}  (37. Kb2  Rxf1 38. Rxf1+ Ke5 39. Kc3 h5 40. Kxd3 Rxh4 41. Re1+ Kd6 42. Re6+ Kc5) Ke5?! {[%eval -2.31] Inaccuracy. Rxf1+ was best.}  (37... Rxf1+  38. Rxf1+ Ke5 39. Kd2 Kd4 40. Rf2 Rxh4 41. c5 Kxc5 42. Kxd3 Rd4+ 43. Kc3) 38. Rxf4?! {[%eval -3.24] Inaccuracy. Rd1 was best.}  (38. Rd1  Kd4 39. c5 Kc3 40. Kb1 Rd4 41. c6 b5 42. Rg7 d2 43. Rg3+ Rd3) Rxf4 {[%eval -3.37] } 39. Rg6?! {[%eval -5.02] Inaccuracy. Kd2 was best.}  (39. Kd2  Kd4 40. Rg2 Rxh4 41. c5 Kxc5 42. Kxd3 Rd4+ 43. Kc3 Rxd5 44. Rg7 Kc6) Kd4 {[%eval -4.84] } 40. d6?! {[%eval -7.08] Inaccuracy. Rg1 was best.}  (40. Rxh6  )  (40. Rg1  Rxh4 41. c5 Kxd5 42. c6 bxc6 43. Rg7 Rh2 44. Rxc7 Rxa2 45. Rg7 Rc2+) Kc3 {[%eval -6.82] }  (40... cxd6  ) 41. Rg1 {[%eval -7.05] } cxd6 {[%eval -6.78] } 42. Rh1 {[%eval -7.08] } h5 {[%eval -6.92] } 43. Kd1 {[%eval -9.12] } Rf2 {[%eval -8.67] good conversion. White had to find Kb2 Kc3 to shoulder my king away from invading while attacking my passed pawn. But he went the wrong, but natural way. } *

